A||||10||TRL46202||Wierd Idle||||1||
Z||000000||TRL46202||12-22-1999||07:28 PM||||My idle jumps between 800 and 900 rpm. I no thats not much but it bugs me, what can I do to fix? Also the car (87GT) is kind of slugish, but not much and when at steady speeds it runs great. Anything I can do to improve this. I have changed the plugs (bosch +4) wires and had the timing checked and set, tranny filter/fluid change and radiator flush it ran a lot better after all that. everything else is a good couple of years old or more. (3rd owner) the second owner did nothing to it in 2yrs.<P>thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sage||12-22-1999||10:05 PM||||Might check your IAC valve.  Don't know if it could cause the problem you've described or not, but also the cat.  Batboy or Ogre or DRH or anybody for that matter!||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||10:16 PM||||Honestly a 100RPM variance at idle is perfectly normal.  I have never been in a computer controlled car that didn't have similar variance at idle.  There is something to be said for perfectionists though.  <P>Good Luck,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000003||Monkeyman||12-23-1999||12:04 AM||||I'd be scared if it moved that much.  Wait...you said "perfectionist", not "paranoid" didn't you?  My bad.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TOM||12-23-1999||12:14 AM||||Mine used to jump like that. But after a bunch of work the idle is rock steady.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||12-23-1999||12:44 AM||||My 86SE does the same thing, but my 86GT is a solid 900 RPM.  One interesting thing is that my SE has an exhaust leak.  It may be the computer trying to compensate for something there.<p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 12-23-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||JetroGT||12-23-1999||01:40 AM||||You could try getting a new coil, and use steel plugs. There is nothing wrong with the Bosch plugs. They will spark under lower voltages because they are platinum, but the spark is not as hot and they make a smaller kernel in the chamber. Steel plugs on the other hand need more voltage to operate correctly but have a much hotter spark and make a bigger kernal.<BR>BTW the kernal is the initial fire ball produced by the spark plug. ||||reg||3
Z||000007||dogtired||12-23-1999||05:12 AM||||Would have to say you are extreamly lucky, if I could keep my idle from fluxuating 8 to 900 I would feel great.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Think I would pull the IAC and clean the pental (the little pointed thing). They sometime carbon up, then you would need to, well it would be a good time to,  reset the ECM and drive it.<BR>Actually I would probably ignore a 100 rpm, difference, actually I have a 8 to 900rpm difference now and I try to ignore it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Good Luck||||reg||6
Z||000008||dogtired||12-23-1999||05:18 AM||||Re-read your post & had another thought. If it is a little slugish, could very well be an injector, starting to build up krud? Might try a can of injector cleaner? Personally I don't like the overhaul in a can junk, but would think a cleaner would be OK?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000009||Carrolles||12-23-1999||06:55 AM||||Sounds like a good candidate for De-carbonizing.  There is a three part system that costs about $40.00 (I got mine from Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory) and it works great.  One can goes in the fuel tank.  One can gets sucked up into a vacuum port and the other is a spray for cleaning the throttlebody.<P>I believe there was a discussion about this some time back.  Ed can be reached at (256) 420-5391 or at Fierofactory@juno.com.<P>Good luck and have a great Christmass and New Year,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000010||TRL46202||12-23-1999||07:37 AM||||Thanks for all the help!||||reg||1
